  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nHigh pressure slides across the Ohio Valley through Friday as\nupper troughing gradually departs to the Northeast. Dry and\nslightly cooler than average conditions will continue through\nthe end of the week. Patchy fog is possible in typical locations\nThursday night into early Friday with radiational cooling.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nAn upper level trough and associated weak surface low will dive\nthrough the western Great Lakes and northern Ohio Valley this\nweekend, bringing a return to increased humidity and unsettled\nweather to the region. Potential for showers and thunder ramps \nup slowly from west to east Friday night and Saturday, with dry \neast winds fighting the potential for rain from eastern OH into\nPA through a good portion of Saturday. There is disagreement \namong the models and ensemble members regarding the finer \ndetails that will determine the eventual timing, coverage, and \namount of rain across the area, though the general consensus is \nthat rain chances will maximize late Saturday into early Sunday.\nA gradual drying trend is then favored from northwest to \nsoutheast Sunday into Sunday night. We are not looking at a \nmulti-day washout this weekend, more like a 1-2 day period with \noccasional shower and storm chances but also intermittent drier \nperiods. Precipitable water values climb to 1.75-2.00\" late \nSaturday into Sunday, so efficient rain-rates and locally heavy \nrainfall may become concerns. However, the general setup appears\nunfavorable for severe thunderstorms with only modest amounts \nof instability and wind shear expected. \n\nHigh pressure pressing in from the north should lead to a\ngeneral drying trend for Monday and Tuesday. There is some \ndisagreement regarding if enough warmth/humidity will persist \nfor lower chances of pop-up showers/storms Monday or Tuesday \nafternoon...mainly farther south across the area. A somewhat \nmore active pattern may return for the second half of next week.\nTemperatures will generally be near to a bit warmer than normal\nthis weekend into early next week.",
